            <ArticleTitle>Epidural analgesia for treatment of a sickle cell crisis during pregnancy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>495-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/AOG.0b013e3182267e58</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">More than 50% of obstetric patients with sickle cell disease will have a pain crisis during pregnancy, and the management of these cases can be challenging.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 20-year-old African American with sickle cell disease presented at 29 4/7 weeks of gestation with severe, debilitating leg and back pain. Large doses of intravenous narcotics did not result in significant pain relief, so a lumbar epidural was placed. This resulted in complete pain relief within several minutes. The patient's symptoms resolved over several days and after a short course of narcotics she was discharged to home, and the remainder of her pregnancy was uncomplicated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Epidural anesthesia should be considered as a potentially effective treatment for a severe sickle cell crisis in obstetric patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
